## Dynamic Routing with Route Parameters

Many pages rely on dynamic data—like a product page that shows details based on an ID. React Router supports **route parameters** via URL segments prefixed with `:`.

### Example: Product Detail Page

```jsx
// src/App.js
function App() {
  return (
    <div>
      <Navbar />
      <Routes>
        <Route path="/" element={<Home />} />
        <Route path="/about" element={<About />} />
        <Route path="/products" element={<Products />} />
        <Route path="/products/:productId" element={<ProductDetail />} />
      </Routes>
    </div>
  );
}
```

```jsx
// src/components/ProductDetail.js
import { useParams } from 'react-router-dom';

function ProductDetail() {
  const { productId } = useParams();

  // In a real app, fetch product data using productId
  return (
    <div>
      <h2>Product Details</h2>
      <p>Showing product with ID: <strong>{productId}</strong></p>
    </div>
  );
}

export default ProductDetail;
```

- `useParams()` is a hook that returns an object of key-value pairs from the URL.
- You can now build dynamic UIs based on the URL.

> 🌐 Real-world use: An e-commerce site might use `/products/nike-air-zoom` where `nike-air-zoom` is a slug mapped to a product ID in the database.

