## Performance Considerations and Anti-Patterns

While Context is powerful, misuse leads to performance bottlenecks.

### ❌ Anti-Pattern: Overusing a Single Context

Putting *everything* into one giant context violates the Single Responsibility Principle and causes massive re-renders.

> ✅ Fix: Split contexts by domain—`AuthContext`, `CartContext`, `ThemeContext`.

### ❌ Anti-Pattern: Passing Unstable Values

Recreating objects or functions in `value` on every render triggers consumer re-renders.

```jsx
// Bad
<CartContext.Provider value={{ cart, setCart }}>...</CartContext.Provider>

// Good
const value = useMemo(() => ({ cart, setCart }), [cart]);
<CartContext.Provider value={value}>...</CartContext.Provider>
```

### ❌ Anti-Pattern: Deeply Nested Providers

Avoid nesting multiple Providers like:

```jsx
<AuthProvider>
  <CartProvider>
    <ThemeProvider>
      <App />
    </ThemeProvider>
  </CartProvider>
</AuthProvider>
```

This creates a "provider hell" and makes testing harder.

> ✅ Fix: Combine providers using compound components or a custom root provider.

```jsx
function AppProviders({ children }) {
  return (
    <AuthProvider>
      <CartProvider>
        <ThemeProvider>
          {children}
        </ThemeProvider>
      </CartProvider>
    </AuthProvider>
  );
}
```

