## Handling Loading, Error, and Success States

Building robust UIs means respecting the user’s time and attention. Never let your app freeze or show broken interfaces.

### State Machine Pattern

Think of your data-fetching flow as a state machine:

- **Initial**: No data, not loading
- **Loading**: Fetching data
- **Success**: Data arrived
- **Error**: Something went wrong

```jsx
const [status, setStatus] = useState('idle'); // 'idle' | 'loading' | 'success' | 'error'

useEffect(() => {
  const fetchData = async () => {
    setStatus('loading');
    try {
      const res = await axios.get('/api/items');
      setItems(res.data);
      setStatus('success');
    } catch (err) {
      setError(err.message);
      setStatus('error');
    }
  };

  fetchData();
}, []);
```

Then render based on `status`:

```jsx
if (status === 'loading') return <Spinner />;
if (status === 'error') return <ErrorAlert message={error} />;
if (status === 'success') return <ItemList items={items} />;
```

> ✅ This pattern scales well and avoids `loading` and `error` state variables cluttering your component.

---

## Caching API Responses

Repeatedly fetching the same data wastes bandwidth and slows down your app. Caching is essential for performance.

### In-Memory Cache with `useRef`

```jsx
import { useState, useEffect, useRef } from 'react';
import axios from 'axios';

function ProductList() {
  const [products, setProducts] = useState([]);
  const [loading, setLoading] = useState(false);
  const [error, setError] = useState(null);
  const cache = useRef({});

  useEffect(() => {
    const cacheKey = 'products';
    if (cache.current[cacheKey]) {
      setProducts(cache.current[cacheKey]);
      return;
    }

    const fetchProducts = async () => {
      setLoading(true);
      try {
        const res = await axios.get('https://api.example.com/products');
        cache.current[cacheKey] = res.data;
        setProducts(res.data);
      } catch (err) {
        setError(err.message);
      } finally {
        setLoading(false);
      }
    };

    fetchProducts();
  }, []);

  // ... render
}
```

> 📌 **Limitation**: This cache disappears on page refresh. For persistence, consider `localStorage` or a state manager.

---

### Cache with Expiration (TTL)

Add time-to-live to prevent stale data:

```jsx
const cache = useRef({
  products: { data: null, expires: null }
});

const fetchWithCache = async (url, ttl = 60000) => {
  const now = Date.now();
  const cached = cache.current[url];

  if (cached && cached.expires > now) {
    return cached.data;
  }

  const res = await axios.get(url);
  cache.current[url] = {
    data: res.data,
    expires: now + ttl
  };
  return res.data;
};
```

> ⏱️ Use `ttl` values appropriate to your data: 10s for stock prices, 1h for blog posts.

