## Role-Based Access Control in Practice

### 1. The Authorization Provider Pattern

Create a context that makes roles available throughout your app:

```javascript
// src/contexts/AuthContext.jsx
import React, { createContext, useContext, useEffect, useState } from 'react';
import authService from '../services/auth.service';

const AuthContext = createContext(null);

export const AuthProvider = ({ children }) => {
  const [user, setUser] = useState(null);
  const [loading, setLoading] = useState(true);

  useEffect(() => {
    const storedUser = authService.getCurrentUser();
    setUser(storedUser);
    setLoading(false);
  }, []);

  const login = async (credentials) => {
    const response = await authService.login(credentials.email, credentials.password);
    setUser(response.user);
    return response;
  };

  const logout = () => {
    authService.logout();
    setUser(null);
  };

  return (
    <AuthContext.Provider value={{ user, login, logout, loading }}>
      {children}
    </AuthContext.Provider>
  );
};

export const useAuth = () => useContext(AuthContext);
```

### 2. Conditional UI Based on Roles

Use the context to show/hide elements:

```javascript
// src/components/AdminMenu.jsx
import { useAuth } from '../contexts/AuthContext';

const AdminMenu = () => {
  const { user } = useAuth();

  if (user?.role !== 'admin') return null;

  return (
    <nav className="admin-nav">
      <Link to="/admin">Dashboard</Link>
      <Link to="/admin/users">User Management</Link>
    </nav>
  );
};
```

### 3. Role-Based API Requests

Modify your API service to include the token:

```javascript
// src/services/api.service.js
import axios from 'axios';
import authService from './auth.service';

const api = axios.create({
  baseURL: 'https://your-api.com',
});

api.interceptors.request.use((config) => {
  const token = authService.getAccessToken();
  if (token) {
    config.headers.Authorization = `Bearer ${token}`;
  }
  return config;
});

api.interceptors.response.use(
  (response) => response,
  async (error) => {
    const originalRequest = error.config;
    if (error.response?.status === 401 && !originalRequest._retry) {
      originalRequest._retry = true;
      await tokenRefreshService.refreshToken();
      return api(originalRequest);
    }
    return Promise.reject(error);
  }
);

export default api;
```

## Advanced Patterns and Pitfalls

### 1. Secure Token Storage Strategies

While localStorage is convenient, it's vulnerable to XSS attacks. Consider:

**Option A: HttpOnly Cookies (Recommended for production)**
- Server sets HttpOnly, Secure, SameSite=Strict cookies
- Client never sees the token
- Requires backend support

**Option B: Session Storage**
- Cleared when tab closes
- Still vulnerable to XSS but less persistent

**Option C: Memory Storage (Short-lived apps)**
- Store token only in React state
- Lost on page refresh
- Requires auto-login

### 2. Token Expiration and Silent Refresh

Implement a token refresh mechanism that:
- Checks expiration before making requests
- Silently refreshes when expired
- Handles refresh token rotation securely

```javascript
// src/services/tokenRefresh.service.js (expanded)
let refreshPromise = null;

export const refreshAccessToken = async () => {
  if (refreshPromise) return refreshPromise;

  refreshPromise = axios.post(`${API_URL}/refresh`, {
    refreshToken: localStorage.getItem('refreshToken')
  })
  .then(response => {
    localStorage.setItem('accessToken', response.data.accessToken);
    refreshPromise = null;
    return response.data.accessToken;
  })
  .catch(error => {
    refreshPromise = null;
    throw error;
  });

  return refreshPromise;
};
```

### 3. Error Handling for Authentication

Create specific error types:

```javascript
// src/utils/authErrors.js
export const AuthErrorTypes = {
  TOKEN_EXPIRED: 'TOKEN_EXPIRED',
  INSUFFICIENT_PERMISSIONS: 'INSUFFICIENT_PERMISSIONS',
  AUTH_FAILED: 'AUTH_FAILED',
};

export class AuthError extends Error {
  constructor(type, message) {
    super(message);
    this.type = type;
    this.name = 'AuthError';
  }
}
```

Then handle them in your API interceptor:

```javascript
api.interceptors.response.use(null, (error) => {
  if (error.response?.status === 401) {
    throw new AuthError(AuthErrorTypes.TOKEN_EXPIRED, 'Session expired');
  }
  if (error.response?.status === 403) {
    throw new AuthError(AuthErrorTypes.INSUFFICIENT_PERMISSIONS, 'Access denied');
  }
  return Promise.reject(error);
});
```

## Real-World Implementation Examples

### Example 1: Building an Admin Dashboard

Create a dashboard that only shows data appropriate for admin roles:

```javascript
// src/pages/AdminDashboard.jsx
import { useEffect, useState } from 'react';
import { useAuth } from '../contexts/AuthContext';
import api from '../services/api.service';
import { AuthError, AuthErrorTypes } from '../utils/authErrors';

const AdminDashboard = () => {
  const { user } = useAuth();
  const [stats, setStats] = useState(null);
  const [loading, setLoading] = useState(true);
  const [error, setError] = useState(null);

  useEffect(() => {
    const fetchStats = async () => {
      try {
        const response = await api.get('/admin/stats');
        setStats(response.data);
      } catch (err) {
        if (err instanceof AuthError && err.type === AuthErrorTypes.INSUFFICIENT_PERMISSIONS) {
          setError('You do not have permission to view this page');
        } else {
          setError('Failed to load dashboard');
        }
      } finally {
        setLoading(false);
      }
    };

    fetchStats();
  }, []);

  if (loading) return <div>Loading...</div>;
  if (error) return <div className="error">{error}</div>;
  if (user?.role !== 'admin') return <Navigate to="/" replace />;

  return (
    <div className="admin-dashboard">
      <h1>Admin Dashboard</h1>
      <div className="stats-grid">
        <div className="stat-card">
          <h3>Total Users</h3>
          <p>{stats.totalUsers}</p>
        </div>
        <div classNameName="stat-card">
          <h3>Active Sessions</h3>
          <p>{stats.activeSessions}</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  );
};
```

### Example 2: Role-Based Navigation

Implement a navigation component that adapts to user roles:

```javascript
// src/components/AppNavigation.jsx
import { NavLink } from 'react-router-dom';
import { useAuth } from '../contexts/AuthContext';

const AppNavigation = () => {
  const { user, logout } = useAuth();

  const navItems = [
    { path: '/', label: 'Home' },
    ...(user ? [
      { path: '/profile', label: 'My Profile' },
      ...(user.role === 'admin' ? [
        { path: '/admin', label: 'Admin Panel' }
      ] : [])
    ] : [
      { path: '/login', label: 'Login' }
    ])
  ];

  return (
    <nav className="app-nav">
      {navItems.map(item => (
        <NavLink key={item.path} to={item.path} end>
          {item.label}
        </NavLink>
      ))}
      {user && (
        <button onClick={logout} className="logout-btn">
          Logout
        </button>
      )}
    </nav>
  );
};
```

### Example 3: Form Submission with Authorization

Handle form submissions that require specific roles:

```javascript
// src/components/UserManagement.jsx
import { useState } from 'react';
import { useAuth } from '../contexts/AuthContext';
import api from '../services/api.service';

const UserManagement = ({ userId, onUpdate }) => {
  const { user } = useAuth();
  const [formData, setFormData] = useState({});
  const [error, setError] = useState(null);
  const [loading, setLoading] = useState(false);

  const handleSubmit = async (e) => {
    e.preventDefault();

    // Authorization check before submission
    if (user.role !== 'admin' && user.id !== userId) {
      setError('You can only edit your own profile');
      return;
    }

    setLoading(true);
    try {
      await api.patch(`/users/${userId}`, formData);
      onUpdate();
    } catch (err) {
      setError('Failed to update profile');
    } finally {
      setLoading(false);
    }
  };

  if (user.role !== 'admin' && user.id !== userId) {
    return <div>You don't have permission to edit this profile</div>;
  }

  return (
    <form onSubmit={handleSubmit}>
      <input
        type="text"
        value={formData.name || ''}
        onChange={(e) => setFormData({...formData, name: e.target.value})}
      />
      <button type="submit" disabled={loading}>
        {loading ? 'Saving...' : 'Save Changes'}
      </button>
      {error && <div className="error">{error}</div>}
    </form>
  );
};
```

## Testing Your Authentication Flow

### 1. Manual Testing Scenarios

Test these critical paths:

1. **Unauthenticated access**: Try accessing `/profile` without logging in
2. **Role switching**: Login as admin, then regular user (requires multiple accounts)
3. **Token expiration**: Set token to expire immediately and verify refresh logic
4. **Session timeout**: Leave app idle and verify silent refresh
5. **Logout behavior**: Verify tokens are cleared and user state resets

### 2. Automated Testing Approach

Write tests for your auth service and protected routes:

```javascript
// src/tests/auth.service.test.js
import authService from '../services/auth.service';
import axios from 'axios';

jest.mock('axios');

describe('AuthService', () => {
  beforeEach(() => {
    localStorage.clear();
  });

  test('login stores tokens and user data', async () => {
    const mockResponse = {
      data: {
        accessToken: 'test-token',
        user: { id: 1, name: 'Test User', role: 'user' }
      }
    };

    axios.post.mockResolvedValue(mockResponse);

    await authService.login('test@example.com', 'password');

    expect(localStorage.getItem('accessToken')).toBe('test-token');
    expect(localStorage.getItem('user')).toBe(JSON.stringify(mockResponse.data.user));
  });

  test('logout clears storage', () => {
    localStorage.setItem('accessToken', 'test-token');
    localStorage.setItem('user', JSON.stringify({ name: 'Test' }));

    authService.logout();

    expect(localStorage.getItem('accessToken')).toBeNull();
    expect(localStorage.getItem('user')).toBeNull();
  });
});
```

## Security Considerations in Production

### 1. Common Vulnerabilities to Address

- **Cross-Site Scripting (XSS)**: Never store tokens in localStorage if your app accepts user-generated content
- **Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF)**: Implement CSRF tokens for state-changing requests
- **Token Theft**: Use short-lived tokens and implement refresh token rotation
- **Information Disclosure**: Don't include sensitive data in JWT payloads
- **Weak Password Policies**: Enforce strong passwords and consider rate limiting

### 2. Environment Configuration

Never hardcode API URLs or secrets:

```javascript
// src/config.js
const config = {
  apiUrl: process.env.REACT_APP_API_URL || 'http://localhost:3001',
  auth: {
    tokenRefreshThreshold: parseInt(process.env.REACT_APP_TOKEN_REFRESH_THRESHOLD) || 30,
  },
  features: {
    adminDashboard: process.env.REACT_APP_ADMIN_DASHBOARD_ENABLED === 'true',
  },
};

export default config;
```

### 3. Monitoring and Logging

Implement basic auth monitoring:

```javascript
// src/services/authMonitor.service.js
import api from './api.service';

export const logAuthEvent = async (eventType, metadata = {}) => {
  try {
    await api.post('/auth/logs', {
      eventType,
      timestamp: new Date().toISOString(),
      userId: api.defaults.headers.common['Authorization'] ? 'authenticated' : 'anonymous',
      ...metadata
    });
  } catch (error) {
    // Don't throw - logging shouldn't break the app
    console.error('Failed to log auth event:', error.message);
  }
};
```

## Key Takeaways

- **Authentication** verifies user identity while **authorization** restricts access based on roles
- Implement a **token refresh mechanism** to handle expired tokens without disrupting user experience
- Use **ProtectedRoute components** to control access to specific routes based on authentication status
- **Role-based access control** should be implemented both at the route level and within components
- **Token storage** requires careful consideration—localStorage is convenient but vulnerable to XSS
- **Error handling** should distinguish between authentication errors and authorization errors
- **Testing** should cover both success and failure paths for authentication flows
- **Security** in production requires addressing XSS, CSRF, token theft, and information disclosure
- **Environment configuration** should externalize API URLs and feature flags
- **Monitoring** auth events helps detect suspicious activity without compromising user experience

## Why Testing Matters in React Development

Imagine deploying a React app where a simple button click crashes the entire application because a prop wasn’t handled correctly. Or worse—your e-commerce checkout component silently fails to process payments because an API mock didn’t account for a new edge case. These aren’t hypotheticals; they’re real risks that testing helps mitigate. React’s declarative nature makes components predictable, but predictability doesn’t equal correctness. Without tests, even small changes can introduce regressions that ripple through your app, eroding user trust and increasing debugging time.

This chapter shifts your focus from *building* React components to *ensuring* they work as intended. You’ll learn to set up a robust testing environment, write tests for components and hooks, mock external dependencies, and use snapshots to catch unintended changes. By the end, you’ll treat testing as a natural part of development—not an afterthought.

---

## Setting Up Jest and React Testing Library

Before writing tests, you need the right tools. Jest handles test execution, assertions, and mocking, while React Testing Library (RTL) provides utilities to interact with components the way users do.

### Installing Dependencies

Create a new React project (or use an existing one) and install the necessary packages:

```bash
npm install --save-dev @testing-library/react @testing-library/jest-dom @testing-library/user-event jest
```

- **@testing-library/react**: Provides utilities to render components in a test environment.
- **@testing-library/jest-dom**: Adds custom matchers like `.toBeInTheDocument()` for assertions.
- **@testing-library/user-event**: Simulates user interactions more accurately than `fireEvent`.
- **jest**: The test runner (included if you’re using Create React App, but install it explicitly for manual setups).

### Configuring Jest

Jest works out of the box with Create React App, but for custom setups, create a `jest.config.js` file:

```javascript
module.exports = {
  testEnvironment: "jsdom",
  setupFilesAfterEnv: ["<rootDir>/src/setupTests.js"],
  moduleNameMapper: {
    "\\.(css|less|scss|sass)$": "identity-obj-proxy",
  },
  transform: {
    "^.+\\.(js|jsx)$": "babel-jest",
  },
};
```

- **testEnvironment**: `jsdom` simulates a browser environment for testing.
- **setupFilesAfterEnv**: Points to a file that runs before tests (e.g., to extend expect with custom matchers).
- **moduleNameMapper**: Mocks CSS imports (using `identity-obj-proxy`) to avoid errors.
- **transform**: Uses Babel to transpile JavaScript/JSX.

### Extending Expect with Custom Matchers

Create `src/setupTests.js` to add RTL’s DOM matchers:

```javascript
import "@testing-library/jest-dom";
```

Now you can write assertions like:

```javascript
expect(button).toBeInTheDocument();
expect(button).toHaveTextContent("Submit");
```

