## Programmatic Navigation with `useNavigate`

Sometimes you need to navigate after an action—like submitting a form or handling an error.

### Example: Login Form with Redirect

```jsx
// src/components/Login.js
import { useState } from 'react';
import { useNavigate } from 'react-router-dom';

function Login() {
  const [email, setEmail] = useState('');
  const [password, setPassword] = useState('');
  const navigate = useNavigate();

  const handleSubmit = (e) => {
    e.preventDefault();
    // In a real app: validate credentials
    console.log('Logging in with:', email);
    // Simulate successful login
    navigate('/dashboard'); // Redirect after login
  };

  return (
    <form onSubmit={handleSubmit}>
      <input
        type="email"
        value={email}
        onChange={(e) => setEmail(e.target.value)}
        placeholder="Email"
      />
      <input
        type="password"
        value={password}
        onChange={(e) => setPassword(e.target.value)}
        placeholder="Password"
      />
      <button type="submit">Login</button>
    </form>
  );
}

export default Login;
```

- `useNavigate()` returns a function to navigate programmatically.
- You can pass a path (`/dashboard`) or go back/forward (`-1`, `1`).

> 🔁 Alternative: You can also use `<Navigate>` for conditional redirects (e.g., after login state is set).

