## Querying GraphQL APIs

GraphQL lets clients request exactly the data they need. Use `fetch` or `axios` with the `Content-Type: application/json` header and send a `query` in the body.

### Example: Fetching Posts with GraphQL

```jsx
import { useState, useEffect } from 'react';

function PostList() {
  const [posts, setPosts] = useState([]);
  const [loading, setLoading] = useState(true);
  const [error, setError] = useState(null);

  useEffect(() => {
    const query = `
      query {
        posts {
          id
          title
          author {
            name
          }
        }
      }
    `;

    fetch('https://your-graphql-api.com/graphql', {
      method: 'POST',
      headers: {
        'Content-Type': 'application/json',
      },
      body: JSON.stringify({ query }),
    })
      .then(response => response.json())
      .then(({ data, errors }) => {
        if (errors) throw new Error(errors[0].message);
        setPosts(data.posts);
      })
      .catch(err => setError(err.message))
      .finally(() => setLoading(false));
  }, []);

  // ... render
}
```

> 🔧 For production, consider using a GraphQL client like **Apollo Client** or **Relay**, which offer advanced caching, subscriptions, and dev tools.

