## ## 2. Preventing Re-renders: Memoization with `React.memo` and `useCallback`

React re-renders components when state or props change. But not all changes are equal. A parent re-rendering doesn’t always mean all children need to re-render. Unfortunately, React doesn’t know that—unless you tell it.

That’s where **memoization** comes in: caching the result of a computation so it doesn’t run again unnecessarily.

### 2.1. `React.memo`: Preventing Unnecessary Re-renders

`React.memo` is a higher-order component that memoizes a functional component. It only re-renders when its props change.

```jsx
const ExpensiveComponent = ({ title, data }) => {
  console.log('ExpensiveComponent rendered'); // Only logs on mount or prop change
  return <h2>{title}: {data.length}</h2>;
};

// Wrap with memo
const MemoizedExpensive = React.memo(ExpensiveComponent);
```

Now, if the parent re-renders but `title` and `data` are the same, `ExpensiveComponent` won’t re-render.

> ✅ **When to use `React.memo`**:
> - The component renders often.
> - It’s expensive to render (e.g., complex calculations, large lists).
> - It receives the same props frequently.
> - It doesn’t rely on context or parent state that changes often.

> ❌ **When NOT to use `React.memo`**:
> - The component is simple and fast.
> - It relies on context that changes often.
> - Its props are objects/arrays that are recreated on every render.

### 2.2. Deep Props and Shallow Comparison

`React.memo` uses **shallow comparison** on props. That means:

```jsx
{ user: { name: 'Alice', id: 1 } }
{ user: { name: 'Alice', id: 1 } } // Same object reference? No → re-render
```

Even if the content is identical, if the object is a new instance, it will trigger a re-render.

```jsx
const Parent = () => {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);
  const user = { name: 'Alice', id: 1 }; // Recreated on every render

  return (
    <div>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>Increment: {count}</button>
      <ExpensiveComponent user={user} />
    </div>
  );
};
```

Here, `user` is recreated on every render, so `React.memo` won’t prevent re-renders.

### 2.3. Fixing with `useMemo` (for values) and `useCallback` (for functions)

To prevent unnecessary re-renders caused by recreated objects or functions, use:

- **`useMemo`**: Memoize expensive calculations.
- **`useCallback`**: Memoize functions so they don’t change on every render.

```jsx
const Parent = () => {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  const user = useMemo(() => ({ name: 'Alice', id: 1 }), []);

  const handleClick = useCallback(() => {
    console.log('Clicked', user.name);
  }, [user]);

  return (
    <div>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>Increment: {count}</button>
      <ExpensiveComponent user={user} onClick={handleClick} />
    </div>
  );
};
```

Now, `user` and `handleClick` maintain stable references, so `ExpensiveComponent` only re-renders when `count` changes.

> 🔁 **Rule of Thumb**: Use `useCallback` when passing a function to a memoized child. Use `useMemo` when computing a value that’s used in props or JSX.

### 2.4. Real-World Example: Expensive List Item

Consider a list where each item runs a heavy computation:

```jsx
const Item = ({ value }) => {
  const processed = expensiveProcess(value); // Runs on every render
  return <li>{processed}</li>;
};

const List = ({ items }) => {
  return (
    <ul>
      {items.map(item => <Item key={item.id} value={item.value} />)}
    </ul>
  );
};
```

Even if `items` doesn’t change, `Item` re-renders because `List` re-renders. And `expensiveProcess` runs again.

Fix:

```jsx
const Item = React.memo(({ value }) => {
  const processed = expensiveProcess(value);
  return <li>{processed}</li>;
}, (prevProps, nextProps) => {
  return prevProps.value === nextProps.value; // Custom comparison
});
```

Now `Item` only re-renders if `value` changes.

> 🧠 **Advanced Tip**: You can provide a custom comparison function as the second argument to `React.memo` for fine-grained control.

