## ## 3. Rendering Lists Efficiently: Keys, Windowing, and Avoiding Reflows

Lists are the backbone of many React apps—dashboards, feeds, tables. But when the list grows, performance tanks.

Why?

- Every item re-renders when the list changes.
- DOM operations (insert, remove, update) are expensive.
- Layout recalculations (reflow) cause jank.

### 3.1. The Problem with Keys

Keys help React identify which items changed, were added, or removed. But a bad key strategy can hurt performance.

✅ **Good key**: Stable, unique, and not related to array index.

```jsx
// Bad: Array index as key (can cause bugs and performance issues)
{items.map((item, index) => <Item key={index} item={item} />)}
```

❌ **Why array index is bad**:
- When items are reordered, React thinks they’re the same.
- Can cause unnecessary re-renders or DOM updates.

✅ **Better**: Use a unique ID.

```jsx
{items.map(item => <Item key={item.id} item={item} />)}
```

### 3.2. Virtualization: Render Only What’s Visible

For long lists (e.g., 1,000+ items), rendering all at once is wasteful. Instead, render only the items visible in the viewport.

Use libraries like:

- `react-window` (formerly `react-virtualized`)
- `react-virtual`

```jsx
import { FixedSizeList as List } from 'react-window';

const Row = ({ index, style }) => (
  <div style={style}>
    Row {index}
  </div>
);

function VirtualList() {
  return (
    <List
      height={400}
      width={300}
      itemCount={1000}
      itemSize={35}
    >
      {Row}
    </List>
  );
}
```

`react-window` renders only ~10 rows at a time, even for 10,000 items. Scrolling is buttery smooth.

> 📏 **Rule**: Use virtualization when:
> - List has 50+ items.
> - Items are similar in height.
> - You care about scroll performance.

### 3.3. Real-World Example: Product Catalog with 2000 Items

```jsx
import { VariableSizeList as List } from 'react-window';

// Each product card has dynamic height
const ProductCard = ({ data, index, style }) => {
  const product = data[index];
  return (
    <div style={style}>
      <img src={product.image} alt={product.name} />
      <h3>{product.name}</h3>
      <p>${product.price}</p>
    </div>
  );
};

function ProductCatalog({ products }) {
  return (
    <List
      height={600}
      width="100%"
      itemCount={products.length}
      itemSize={(index) => getHeightForProduct(products[index])}
      itemData={products}
    >
      {ProductCard}
    </List>
  );
}
```

Even with 2,000 products, scrolling feels instant. Without virtualization, the app would freeze on scroll.

> 🛠️ **Tip**: Pair virtualization with `React.memo` for each row to prevent re-renders when unrelated state changes.

